  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:30 AM EDT...Clear skies and calm winds \nacross the N/NE third of our region allowed for temperatures to \ndrop into the low 40s with patchy fog earlier tonight, but \nadvancing mid-level cloud shield seen on GOES 16 nighttime \nmicrophysics RGB satellite product is keeping temperatures much \nwarmer (mid 50s) for the rest of the region. Temps should pretty\nmuch hold steady through the rest of the night. \n\nWhile current radar shows some very light scattered showers across \nthe region, most of this is not reaching the ground due to low/mid-\nlevel dry air. However, shower coverage will increase towards \nsunrise as a inverted sfc trough currently located over Ohio forms a \nclosed sfc low and tracks across PA later today. Strengthening LLJ \nwith this system will help lift a mid-level warm front across the \nsouthern portion of our region, although the sfc front remains to \nour south. Surging low to mid-level moisture, already seen to our \nwest on CIRA ALPW satellite imagery, will be lifted up and over the \nfront, resulting in widespread stratiform rain across the region for \nmost of the day. The right entrance of the upper jet to our north \nwill also help to enhance lift and allow the precip shield to spread \nnorthwards. Highs today will struggle to make it out of the 50s for \nmost areas.\n\nTotal rainfall amounts have come down slightly since the previous \nforecast, but a general half inch to inch is expected for most of \nthe area, with the higher amounts across the Catskills, Mid \nHudson Valley, and western CT which will be closer to the better\nmid-level warm advection and isentropic lift. Given lack of \ninstability, rainfall rates will only be on the order of 0.1 to \nbriefly 0.25\" per hour, so we are not concerned about any \nflooding issues. In fact, this rainfall will be largely \nbeneficial as much of the region is actually running slightly \nbelow normal for precip over the past 30 days or so. \n\nRain tapers to showers this evening/early tonight as the sfc low \ntracks off to the E/SE of our region and a strong 1030+ mb sfc high \nbuilds into southern Canada. This will help push a cold frontal \nboundary to the south of our region, where it will stall for the \nfirst half of the week. With the sfc high continuing to\nstrengthen beneath confluent flow aloft, mainly dry weather is \nexpected for the first half of the week, although a few showers \ncould reach as far north as southern Ulster/Dutchess/Litchfield \nCounties Tuesday as an upper disturbance tracks south of our \nregion. With persistent E/NE flow, temperatures will run below \nnormal through the first half of the week, giving us a prolonged\ntaste of fall weather. Highs each day will only be in the 50s \nto 60s and lows each night in the 30s to 40s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By the middle to end of the week, anticyclonic \nwave breaking leads to the development of a Rex Block pattern, \nwith a closed upper low sliding to the south of our region. As \nit emerges over the Atlantic, coastal cyclogenesis will occur \nwith the resulting sfc low tracking somewhere off the east \ncoast. Latest guidance now keeps our area dry through the end of\nthe week, with any potential rain from this coastal low delayed\nuntil next weekend. Guidance tends to struggle with these \nblocky flow patterns, and so confidence for this time period \nremains quite low. Additional changes to the forecast late-week \nshould be expected. Nevertheless, given that this potential \ncoastal low looks to be quite moisture- laden, we will continue \nto monitor closely. If we do see any rain from this system, the \nmost likely areas would be the Mid Hudson Valley and/or \nsouthwestern New England.",
